## Summary

I implemented the household-duplicate rejection rule for `POST /api/owners`, following the existing `isTelephoneInUse` conflict-check pattern already in the codebase.

**1. OpenAPI spec** (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`) — added a write-only, optional `sharesHousehold` boolean (default `false`) to the `OwnerFields` schema. The DTOs are generated from this spec at build time, so this gives the controller a `getSharesHousehold()` accessor on `OwnerFieldsDto`.

**2. Service** (`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`) — added `isHouseholdInUse(lastName, address)`. It canonicalizes both the last name and address (trim → collapse whitespace runs to a single space → lower-case) and returns `true` if any existing owner matches on both, mirroring how `isTelephoneInUse` scans `ownerRepository.findAll()`.

**3. Controller** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`) — after the existing telephone check, it now returns `409 CONFLICT` when a household match exists, unless the request set `sharesHousehold: true`:

```java
if (!Boolean.TRUE.equals(ownerFieldsDto.getSharesHousehold())
    && this.clinicService.isHouseholdInUse(owner.getLastName(), owner.getAddress())) {
    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
}
```

This satisfies all three acceptance tests: same last name + address → 409; different address → created; same last name + address with `sharesHousehold: true` → created.
